          "message": "The test passes.\n\n## Change\n\nOnly `app/parser.py` was modified.\n\n**Root cause:** `parse_date` is documented to accept both `YYYY-MM-DD` and `YYYY/MM/DD`, but the splitting logic only ever split on `-`. So `\"2026/12/31\"` came back as a single chunk and the tuple unpacking raised `ValueError: not enough values to unpack (expected 3, got 1)`.\n\nA detail worth flagging: `split_date` is imported from `shared.utils` when available, with a local fallback used when `shared/` is absent. In this checkout `shared/` does not exist, so the fallback is what actually runs \u2014 that is where the `-`-only split lived.\n\n**Fix:** normalise `/` to `-` (and strip whitespace) before splitting. I did this in two places:\n\n- In the fallback `split_date`, so it honours both documented formats.\n- In `parse_date` itself, before calling `split_date`. This matters because I can't see `shared/utils.py` in this checkout and have no way to confirm whether the real helper handles `/`. Normalising at the call site means `parse_date` behaves identically on either code path, rather than passing in production and only in this checkout.\n\nThe second point is the one to sanity-check against a full checkout. If `shared.utils.split_date` already handles `/`, my normalisation is a harmless no-op. If it has other behaviour I can't see (e.g. accepting `YYYY.MM.DD`, or rejecting `-`), the pre-normalisation could mask or alter it \u2014 worth a glance from someone with `shared/` available. The alternative, fixing only the fallback, would have left the test's pass/fail status dependent on whether `shared/` happens to be installed, which seemed worse.",
